{"step_index":72,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T13:25:20Z","input_tokens":2845,"cache_read_tokens":70321,"output_tokens":1899,"thinking":"Observing the iOS view hierarchy reveals a `ConversationPanelViewController` managing chat and catch-up components. The user interface includes chat, live text, and catch-up modes via a segmented control, with a scrollable message list and transcript view. Considering the user input \"right now,\" the application's current state and available data must be evaluated to understand the context of this statement.\n\n\nExamining the iOS app, reactions display within the text chat but are absent from the live transcription view, contrasting with desktop behavior. The `ConversationPanelViewController`'s chat mode rendering confirms reaction display using `chat.reactionHistory`, but further investigation is needed for the live transcription component. Determining if a separate rendering or data source is required for the transcription view is the next step.\n\n\nThe code indicates a conditional transcript rendering within `ConversationPanelViewController` tied to `ConversationMode.liveText`. It appears that the transcript relies on `timeline.segments`, specifically handling updates when new segments are available. If no transcript segments are available or if transcription is disabled, a fallback message is displayed. Comparing the logic flow, the current hypothesis is transcription mode not being enabled.\n\n\nThe iOS implementation deliberately excludes reactions during transcript rendering, confirmed by the `chat.reactionHistory` subscription explicitly setting `transcript: false`. The `renderTranscript()` function focuses solely on `timeline.segments`, ignoring any reaction data. The desktop application integrates reactions directly into the live text display using a `LiveTextRow` enum that combines both text lines and reactions. This contrast suggests the iOS app needs a similar mechanism to merge reactions with transcript segments.\n\n\nThe desktop implementation merges reactions into the live text using timestamps, specifically including reactions that occur after the first transcript line. The iOS code lacks this integration; it only considers transcript segments, and so reactions that occurred after the beginning of transcription do not appear. This suggests a merging logic, similar to the desktop version, must be implemented on the iOS side to include reactions with transcript lines.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"git grep -n \\\"\\\\.emoji\\\"\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolSummary":"\"Grep emoji in codebase\""}}]}
