sEmpty` reveals a potential flaw: reactions might be present, but the view won't update if `timeline.segments` is empty. The discrepancy is clearer now. Reactions might not render if the meeting transcript segments are empty, even if the meeting is in\n<truncated 186 bytes>\nmentation includes reactions in the live text view even when the transcript is initially empty, but the iOS implementation seems to omit reactions when the transcript is also empty. A test case confirms that the desktop application renders reactions even without an initial transcript. This suggests the iOS view controller logic requires modification to behave the same way. The next step is to make the iOS implementation match the desktop behavior.\n\n\nThe test case asserts two items in `liveTextRows`, confirming the presence of both transcript lines and reactions in the desktop implementation. This contrasts with the iOS implementation, which uses a `UITextView` with `attributedText` for rendering, rather than a SwiftUI list or scroll view. This difference in UI components requires further investigation to ensure consistent display behavior across platforms. The switch from a SwiftUI list or scroll view to a UITextView will require a re-evaluation of the data binding and rendering mechanisms.\n\n\nThe iOS implementation builds the `attributedText` for the `UITextView`. The code concatenates transcript segments into an attributed string, constructing each line with optional timestamps and speaker names. The current rendering logic seems to always build from the `timeline.segments` array and the key formatting is done on each line. It does not appear to have an explicit \"reactions\" branch like the desktop implementation. This highlights a clear gap: the lack of a separate handling path for reactions. Now to check if reactions can be appended like the desktop implementation.\n\n\nThe desktop implementation displays reactions as an HStack with an emoji, display name, and timestamp, formatted as text elements. Focusing on the iOS `UITextView`'s `NSAttributedString`, the reaction could be rendered as attributed text with the emoji and participant name, potentially incorporating time. Verification will be done by looking at how similar views are handled, such as `ReactionHistoryRowView.swift`, and other design documentation.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"git grep -n -C 5 \\\"ReactionHistoryRowView\\\" RockNRoll/\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolSummary":"\"Check ReactionHistoryRowView usage\""}}],"truncated_fields":["thinking"]}
