{"step_index":94,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T13:31:13Z","input_tokens":2429,"cache_read_tokens":92383,"output_tokens":2918,"thinking":"The code points to `NativeConferenceEngine.swift` within the reaction history. It seems to process a reaction event. The desktop app uses `DesktopMeetingModel.swift` to handle `LiveTextRow` events, which include both lines and reactions. This suggests a potential link in how reaction data is managed across platforms.\n\n\nThe `liveTextRows` function on the desktop app merges lines and reactions chronologically. The iOS app utilizes a similar `LiveTextRow` enum, suggesting a shared data structure. The iOS app's `ConversationPanelViewController` renders the transcript using a `UITextView`. The iOS `TranscriptSegment` structure appears comparable to the desktop's `GuestMeetingMessage`, indicating a potential data mapping between the platforms.\n\n\niOS uses a similar `LiveTextRow` enum to combine transcript segments and reaction events, suggesting alignment with the desktop app. The desktop app prioritizes adding transcript lines, then includes reactions if they occurred after the first line's timestamp. The iOS implementation likely shares this ordering logic to merge the data types.\n\n\nThe reaction inclusion logic appears to be time-based, incorporating reactions if their timestamp is after the first transcript line. If no lines exist, all reactions are included. The lines are rendered to the `UITextView` which is shown as `liveTranscript()`. The `liveTranscript` function creates an attributed string combining a disclaimer with the output from `transcriptLines`. The rendering logic uses `transcriptLines`.\n\n\nThe formatting of the desktop's reactions shows the emoji, display name, and timestamp. The equivalent `NSAttributedString` needs to incorporate these elements. The timestamp formatting from the desktop can be adopted. The `NSAttributedString` should construct a similar structure to ensure a consistent presentation between platforms.\n\n\nThe desktop app displays reactions with emoji, participant name, and timestamp, each with specific font and color styling. The equivalent iOS `UITextView` needs corresponding formatting using `NSAttributedString`. The desktop uses a caption font and secondary color for the participant's name and the timestamp. The timestamp uses a shortened time format.\n\nExamining the incremental rendering logic in `renderTranscript()` could help determine how to best append reactions.\n\n\nThe rendering logic compares the existing segments to the new segments to append only the new elements. Suspect the reaction data can arrive interleaved within the segments or appended, requiring an incremental update to the displayed text. The core logic should check if the new segments start with the previously rendered ones. If so, only the new lines need appending. Otherwise, the entire text needs to be re-rendered. Edge cases to consider involve empty segment data with enabled transcription.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"read_file","args":{"EndLine":"50","Path":"\"/Users/v.smirnov/docs/conference-guest-ios/RockNRoll/CatchUpText.swift\"","StartLine":"1"}}]}
{"step_index":95,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T13:32:16Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-10T16:32:16+03:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-10T16:32:16+03:00\nShowing lines 1-26 of 26\n1: import ConferenceCore\n2: import Foundation\n3: \n4: enum CatchUpText {\n5:     static func live(timeline: CatchUpTimeline, canView: Bool?, enabled: Bool) -> String {\n6:         if canView == false { return L(\"Transcripts aren’t available in this room.\") }\n7:         if canView == nil { return L(\"Checking transcript availability…\") }\n8:         if !enabled { return L(\"Transcription is off in this room.\") }\n9:         guard !timeline.segments.isEmpty else { return L(\"Waiting for spoken words…\") }\n10:         let lines = timeline.segments.sorted {\n11:             ($0.spokenAt ?? .distantFuture) < ($1.spokenAt ?? .distantFuture)\n12:         }.map(describe)\n13:         return lines.joined(separator: \"\\n\\n\") +\n14:             (timeline.isTruncated ? L(\"\\n\\nOlder lines have left this phone’s memory.\") : \"\")\n15:     }\n16: \n17:     private static func describe(_ segment: TranscriptSegment) -> String {\n18:         let speaker = segment.speaker?.isEmpty == false ? segment.speaker! : L(\"Participant\")\n19:         let time = segment.spokenAt.map { \"[\\(clock($0))] \" } ?? \"\"\n20:         return \"\\(time)\\(speaker): \\(segment.text)\"\n21:     }\n22: \n23:     private static func clock(_ date: Date) -> String {\n24:         DateFormatter.localizedString(from: date, dateStyle: .none, timeStyle: .short)\n25:     }\n26: }"}
